In uniaxial symmetry, materials exhibit different polarizabilities along the principal axes due to their anisotropic nature. The polarizability tensor can be expressed in a diagonal form, typically represented as:

\[
\mathbf{\alpha} = \begin{pmatrix}
\alpha_{\perp} & 0 & 0 \\
0 & \alpha_{\perp} & 0 \\
0 & 0 & \alpha_{\parallel}
\end{pmatrix}
\]

Here, \(\alpha_{\perp}\) represents the polarizability in the directions perpendicular to the optical axis, while \(\alpha_{\parallel}\) denotes the polarizability along the optical axis. This distinction arises because uniaxial materials have one axis of symmetry (the optical axis) where the properties differ from those in the perpendicular directions. Understanding these forms of polarizabilities is crucial for applications in optics and material science, as they influence the behavior of light in anisotropic media.
